  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nRidging aloft continues to build over the interior west, while a \ntrough approaching the Pacific NW gradually brings a more unstable\nsouthwesterly flow aloft over the Northern Rockies. Widely \nscattered showers and thunderstorms have expanded northward over \nthe last couple of days and will continue to do so through \nThursday. Additionally, thunderstorm coverage and intensity will \nalso be on the increase as shortwaves move through the \nsouthwesterly flow and interact with steep lapse rates above 8C/km\nand ML CAPE in the 300 to 900 J/kg range. Very warm and dry sub-\ncloud layers will make localized strong, gusty winds and lightning\nthe primary concerns with this activity. While most storms will \noccur in the afternoon and evening, there will also be at least \nsome overnight convection. The combination of the very warm and \ndry conditions with scattered thunderstorm activity will also \nbring elevated fire weather conditions to southwest and portions \nof central MT, see the Fire Weather section for more information. \n\nEnsembles are coming into agreement with the trough ejecting\neastward into the Northern Rockies this weekend into early next\nweek. Friday looks to be a transition day, with showers and storms\nbeginning to retreat back to southwest MT and temperatures\nbeginning to trend cooler. The main trough then moves through the\nNorthern Rockies and brings more areawide scattered showers and\nsome thunderstorms, gusty winds, and cooler temperatures. CAPE\nwill be highest on Saturday, but still not as high as earlier in\nthe week. The combination of afternoon temperatures cooling below\naverage with more productive showers should limit severe weather\nand fire weather concerns. In fact, some of the latest most\naggressive deterministics bring well below average temperatures on\nSunday, and even a bit of high mountain snow. The evolution of\nthis system will be closely monitored over the next few days.\nSouthwesterly flow aloft quickly returns to the Northern Rockies\nearly next week as another trough moves onto the Pacific NW coast.\nThis trough looks to swing eastward heading towards the middle and\nend of next week, although its depth and overall impact are\nuncertain at this time. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms today through Friday\u2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will initiate over west and southwest \nMT by mid- afternoon before moving towards the plains and valleys \nto the east later in the afternoon and evening. This activity will\nexpand farther north and increase in coverage and intensity \ncompared to previous days. Forecast soundings show tall inverted V\nprofiles, with DCAPE values in the 1100 to 1400 J/kg range. This \nindicates that strong, gusty winds will be the primary hazard with\nthis activity. This is corroborated by the latest suite of hires \nguidance, which highlight wind gusts in between 40 and 60 mph with\nthis broken line of storms, strongest for locations near and west\nof I15. Hail shouldn\u2019t be a significant problem in this \nenvironment, but with effective shear values approaching and \nexceeding 25 kts, some hail can\u2019t be ruled out with the strongest \ncores. Similar conditions unfold on Thursday, though the forcing \nmay be a bit stronger and the storm coverage maybe a little more \nwidespread. By Friday shower and thunderstorm activity begins to \nretreat to the southwest and ML CAPE values look to fall below 500\nJ/kg in most cases for weaker storm intensity. - RCG",
